#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175771 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37414146-16e2-5190-9fa5-0a7ecafdff22" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Trigonometric Ratio of a Negative Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "9c5acd23-2011-56f9-b1a9-749c7590c53c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3866" :question/problem "$\\cot (-270^\\circ) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-270^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n\n$$\n-270^\\circ + 360^\\circ = 90^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cot\\left(-270^\\circ\\right) = \\cot{90^\\circ}\n$$\n\nThe angle $90^\\circ$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $90^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $(0,1)$.\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos{90^\\circ} = 0$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin{90^\\circ} = 1$.\n\nSo, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\cot{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\cot{90^\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\cos{90^\\circ}}{\\sin{90^\\circ}} = \\dfrac{0}{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cot(-270^\\circ) =0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175772 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74bd8bfc-a69a-5d02-a22e-4f8c2d062111" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio of a Quadrantal Angle Greater Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/id #uuid "3192749c-2bdd-5fb8-a5ca-a5b8238fca84" :question/math-academy-id "e-3867" :question/problem "$\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}\\right) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7π}{2} - 2π &= \\frac{3π}{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}2\\right) = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)\n$$\n\nThe angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point $(0,-1)$.\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)} = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}2\\right) = -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37414146-16e2-5190-9fa5-0a7ecafdff22" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Trigonometric Ratio of a Negative Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ac2b044-1c32-50e9-abfb-70452eb79673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174671} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8c18e1-fb20-5289-8bd3-160372e8e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174672} {:answer/id #uuid "95908aeb-6bd7-5538-b391-ef751bafe30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174673} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb261a8-9bf7-5970-864c-2c9eccb52c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174674} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ee1f2d-cebb-5fb5-a621-cc3e02492655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174675}] :answer-field/id #uuid "18eaf0d2-a4b3-5524-8b0d-9be55c5f2cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174670}] :question/id #uuid "946cf0ed-3494-5355-911d-9370edfcdf68" :question/math-academy-id "q-1693" :question/problem "What is $\\csc (-90^{∘})$?"}]}
